            <ArticleTitle>Reversal of diastolic dysfunction by intravenous magnesium chloride.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>618-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To treat diastolic dysfunction in patients with coronary artery disease (CAD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Open study of the intravenous injection of 5 mL of a solution containing 17% magnesium chloride (equal to 100 mg of elemental magnesium) during routine coronary angiography in patients with a raised left ventricular end-diastolic pressure (LVEDP).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A hospital department of cardiac hemodynamics.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ten patients with angiographically documented CAD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After magnesium chloride injection, the LVEDP was significantly reduced in all patients, from a mean of 24 +/- 3 to 16 +/- 3 mmHg (P &lt; 0.001).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Under the short term conditions of the study, the injection of magnesium chloride effectively improved left ventricular diastolic function. Further study of the long term effect is required.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
